        "question": {
          ":question/id": "008cedc5-9808-5882-af79-313935f8faa0",
          ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
          ":question/problem": "Consider the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={x}^{2}y$ and its slope field. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** At every point in the third quadrant, the slope of the slope field is positive\n- **II.** At every point in the fourth quadrant, the slope of the slope field is positive\n- **III.** At every point in the second quadrant, the slope of the slope field is positive",
          ":question/worked-solution": "First, we define $f(x,y)={x}^{2}y.$ Then, our differential equation is $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(x,y).$ \n\nNotice that $f(x,y)=0$ when $x=0$ or $y=0.$ The lines $x=0$ and $y=0$ divide the plane into the usual four quadrants.\n\nIn an open quadrant, each squared coordinate is positive, so the remaining coordinate factors and coefficient determine the sign.\n\nIf we compute the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each quadrant (by considering some test points, for example), we get the following:\n\n![](images/generated/signs-ppnn-zero-xy.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement:\n\n- Statement I is false. If $(x,y)$ is in the third quadrant, then according to our diagram, we have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,y)}=f(x,y)<0.\n$$\n\nThis implies that the slope of the slope field is negative for any point $(x,y)$ in the third quadrant.\n\n- Statement II is false. If $(x,y)$ is in the fourth quadrant, then according to our diagram, we have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,y)}=f(x,y)<0.\n$$\n\nThis implies that the slope of the slope field is negative for any point $(x,y)$ in the fourth quadrant.\n\n- Statement III is true. If $(x,y)$ is in the second quadrant, then according to our diagram, we have\n\n$$\n{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(x,y)}=f(x,y)>0.\n$$\n\nThis implies that the slope of the slope field is positive for any point $(x,y)$ in the second quadrant.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"",
